    {"step": 1, "tactic": ".sys driver file dropped to user TEMP", "technique": "T1068", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-26 11:18:08 UTC, Sysmon EID 11 reports powershell.exe creating C:\\Users\\r.morrison\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\p97_t01_111808\\vuln_111808.sys (2052 bytes). Custom rule 200304 (.sys drop) triggered.", "chain_reason": "A .sys file in user TEMP is virtually never legitimate — drivers are installed by signed installers under %Program Files% or %SystemRoot%\\System32\\drivers."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "sc.exe create with type=kernel — service registration as kernel driver", "technique": "T1543.003", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-26 11:18:11, Sysmon EID 1 captures sc.exe with cmdline 'sc.exe create p97_byovd_111808 binPath= C:\\Users\\...\\vuln_111808.sys type= kernel start= demand'. Custom rule 200300 + Security EID 4697 (kernel service install) triggered.", "chain_reason": "type=kernel registers a Windows kernel-mode driver service — the canonical BYOVD final step. Required for any sc start to load into the kernel."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "sc start invoked on the new kernel service", "technique": "T1211", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-26 11:18:14, Sysmon EID 1 captures sc.exe with cmdline 'sc.exe start p97_byovd_111808'. Whether the driver actually loads depends on Code Integrity policy + signature trust — sc start logs the attempt regardless.", "chain_reason": "sc start is the kernel-load trigger. On systems with Driver Signature Enforcement, an unsigned/random-bytes .sys will be rejected; on hosts with relaxed CI policy or via a previously-signed vulnerable driver it would actually load."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Sysmon EID 6 driver load event", "technique": "T1211", "what_happened": "At 2032-09-26 11:18:16, Sysmon EID 6 (Driver loaded) may fire with the .sys path + sha256. If hash matches a known LOLDrivers / VulnDrivers list, rule 7030 escalates.", "chain_reason": "EID 6 is the kernel-level confirmation that a driver image was actually mapped — distinguishes a failed load (sc start error, no EID 6) from a successful kernel implant."}
  ],
